---
title: Proposition 23
---
order: 23
---
short_label: XI.23
---
red_highlight: yes
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To construct a solid angles out of three plane angles such that the sum of any two is greater than the remaining one: thus the sum of the three angles must be less than four right angles.
----
proof:

[!just XI.20; XI.21]

Let the angles {ABC|angABC}, {DEF|angDEF}, and {GHK|angGHK} be the three given plane angles, and let the sum of any two of them be greater than the remaining one, and further, let the sum of all three be less than four right angles.

It is required to construct a solid angle out of angles equal to the angles {ABC|angABC}, {DEF|angDEF}, and {GHK|angGHK}.

Cut off {AB}, {BC}, {DE}, {EF}, {GH}, and {HK} equal to one another, and join {AC}, {DF}, and {GK}. [!just I.3]

It is therefore possible to construct a triangle out of straight lines equal to {AC}, {DF}, and {GK}. Construct {LMN|LMNT} so that {AC} equals {LM}, {DF} equals {MN}, and {GK} equals {NL}. [!just XI.22]

<center>
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="600" height="270" tabindex="0"></canvas>
