Describe the circle {LMN|LMNcircT} about the triangle {LMN|LMNT}, and take its center {O}. Join {LO}, {MO}, and {NO}. [!just IV.5, III.1]

I say that {AB} is greater than {LO}.

For, if not, {AB} either equals {LO}, or is less.

First, let it be equal. Then, since {AB} equals {LO}, while {AB} equals {BC}, and {LO} equals {OM}, therefore the two sides {AB} and {BC} equal the two sides {LO} and {OM} respectively. And, by hypothesis, the base {AC} equals the base {LM}, therefore the angle {ABC|angABC} equals the angle {LOM|angLOM}. [!just I.8]

For the same reason the angle {DEF|angDEF} also equals the angle {MON|angMON}, and the angle {GHK|angGHK} equals the angle {NOL|angNOL}. Therefore the sum of the three angles {ABC|angABC}, {DEF|angDEF}, and {GHK|angGHK} equals the sum of the three angles {LOM|angLOM}, {MON|angMON}, and {NOL|angNOL}.

But the sum of the three angles {LOM|angLOM}, {MON|angMON}, and {NOL|angNOL} equals four right angles, therefore the sum of the three angles {ABC|angABC}, {DEF|angDEF}, and {GHK|angGHK} equals four right angles.

But the sum is also, by hypothesis, less than four right angles, which is absurd. Therefore {AB} is not equal to {LO}.

I say next that neither is {AB} less than {LO}.

For, if possible, let it be so. Make {OP} equal to {AB}, and {OQ} equal to {BC}, and join {PQ}. [!just I.3]

Then, since {AB} equals {BC}, therefore {OP} also equals {OQ}, so that the remainder {LP} equals {QM}.

Therefore {LM} is parallel to {PQ}, and {LMO} is equiangular with {PQO}. [!just VI.2, I.29]

Therefore, {OL} is to {LM} as {OP} is to {PQ}, and alternately, {LO} is to {OP} as {LM} is to {PQ}. [!just VI.4, V.16]

But {LO} is greater than {OP}, therefore {LM} is greater than {PQ}. And {LM} equals {AC}, therefore {AC} is greater than {PQ}.

Since, then, the two sides {AB} and {BC} equal the two sides {PO} and {OQ}, and the base {AC} is greater than the base {PQ}, therefore the angle {ABC|angABC} is greater than the angle {POQ|angLOM}. [!just I.25]

Similarly we can prove that the angle {DEF|angDEF} is also greater than the angle {MON|angMON}, and the angle {GHK|angGHK} is greater than the angle {NOL|angNOL}.

Therefore the sum of the three angles {ABC|angABC}, {DEF|angDEF}, and {GHK|angGHK} is greater than the sum of the three angles {LOM|angLOM}, {MON|angMON}, and {NOL|angNOL}. But, by hypothesis, the sum of the angles {ABC|angABC}, {DEF|angDEF}, and {GHK|angGHK} is less than four right angles, therefore the sum of the angles {LOM|angLOM}, {MON|angMON}, and {NOL|angNOL} is much less than four right angles. But the sum also equals four right angles, which is absurd. Therefore {AB} is not less than {LO}.

And it was proved that neither is it equal, therefore {AB} is greater than {LO}.

<div class="just"><a href="/elements/books/bookXI/propositions/propXI12/">XI.12</a><br><a href="#lemma">Lemma below</a></div>

Next set up {OR:canvas_1} from the point {O:canvas_1} at right angles to the plane of the circle {LMN|LMNcircT:canvas_1} so that the square on {OR:canvas_1} equals the square on {AB:canvas_1} minus the square on {LO:canvas_1}. Join {RL:canvas_1}, {RM:canvas_1}, and {RN:canvas_1}.

<center>
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="600" height="270" tabindex="0"></canvas>
