</script>
</center>

Then, since {RO} is at right angles to the plane of the circle {LMN|LMNcircT}, therefore {RO} is also at right angles to each of the straight lines {LO}, {MO}, and {NO}. And, since {LO} equals {OM}, and {OR} is common and at right angles, therefore the base {RL} equals the base {RM}. [!just XI.Def.3; I.4]

For the same reason {RN} also equals each of the straight lines {RL} and {RM}. Therefore the three straight lines {RL}, {RM}, and {RN} equal one another.

Next, since by hypothesis the square on {OR} equals equals the square on {AB} minus the square on {LO}, therefore the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {LO} and {OR}.

But the square on {LR} equals the sum of the squares on {LO} and {OR}, for the angle {LOR|angLOR} is right, therefore the square on {AB} equals the square on {RL}. Therefore {AB} equals {RL}. [!just I.47]

But each of the straight lines {BC}, {DE}, {EF}, {GH}, and {HK} equals {AB}, while each of the straight lines {RM} and {RN} equals {RL}, therefore each of the straight lines {AB}, {BC}, {DE}, {EF}, {GH}, and {HK} equals each of the straight lines {RL}, {RM}, and {RN}.

Since the two sides {LR} and {RM} equal the two sides {AB} and {BC}, and, by hypothesis, the base {LM} equals the base {AC}, therefore the angle {LRM|angLRM} equals the angle {ABC|angABC}. For the same reason the angle {MRN|angMRN} equals the angle {DEF|angDEF}, and the angle {LRN|angLRN} equals the angle {GHK|angGHK}. [!just I.8]

Therefore, out of the three plane angles {LRM|angLRM}, {MRN|angMRN}, and {LRN|angLRN}, which equal the three given angles {ABC|angABC}, {DEF|angDEF}, and {GHK|angGHK}, the solid angle at {R} has been constructed, which is contained by the angles {LRM|angLRM}, {MRN|angMRN}, and {LRN|angLRN}.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>

<a name="lemma"></a>

### Lemma

But how it is possible to take the square on *OR* equal to the square on {AB} minus the square on *LO* we can show as follows.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_2" width="220" height="180" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="XI.23 lemma diagram" src="propXI23l.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's lemma figure: the semicircle on AB and the triangle ACB in it.
    // The lemma's LO and OR are not drawn, so they are left as text.
    //
    // Added, hidden at rest: solid twins ABs, ACs, CBs (initiallyHidden) so
    // AB stands before the semicircle and the fit and the join stroke from
    // A and from C. Added, all transparent: AB, AC, CB for the highlight and
    // the prose; semiT, a faceless twin of the filled semicircle, which
    // would be raised over the triangle if highlighted (#140). The right
    // angle ACB (III.31) is an angleMarkerRight square, hidden at rest. The
    // triangle owns the name ABC, so the semicircle ABC takes the override.
    //
    // Joyce's last three steps have the squares the wrong way round (the
    // square on AB equals the square on AC minus the square on CB, and so
    // on); they are left literal.
    //
    // Draggables (checked against the bundle): A, B, C. C arrives with
    // "Fit AC", so it is deferred.
    var LG   = ["A","B","ABs","AB"];
    var LS   = LG.concat(["semi","semiT"]);
    var LFIT = LS.concat(["C","ACs","AC"]);
    var LJN  = LFIT.concat(["CBs","CB","ABC"]);
    var LRT  = LJN.concat(["angACB"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_2",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "XI.23, Lemma",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;40,140",
            "B;point;free;180,140",
            "O;point;midpoint;A,B;0;0",
            "circ;circle;radius;O,A;0;0;0;0",
            "C;point;circleSlider;circ,80,40",
            "semi;sector;arc;B,C,A;0;0;black;random",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;black;random",
            // Slide twins, hidden at rest.
            "ABs;line;connect;A,B",
            "ACs;line;connect;A,C",
            "CBs;line;connect;C,B",
            // Transparent highlight targets.
            "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;0",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C;0;0;0",
            "CB;line;connect;C,B;0;0;0",
            "semiT;sector;arc;B,C,A;0;0;0;0",
            "angACB;sector;angleMarkerRight;C,A,B"
        ],
        initiallyHidden: ["ABs","ACs","CBs"],
        deferDraggables: ["C"],
        aliases: { "BC": "CB" },
        slides: [
            { text: "But how it is possible to take the square on OR",
              visible: LG,
              highlighted: ["AB"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Lemma" }] },
            { text: "equal to the square on {AB} minus the square on LO we can show as follows.",
              visible: LG,
              highlighted: ["AB"] },
            { text: "Set out the straight lines {AB} and LO, and let {AB} be the greater.",
              visible: LG,
              highlighted: ["AB"] },
            { text: "Describe the semicircle {ABC|semiT} on {AB}.",
              visible: LS,
              highlighted: ["semiT","AB"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "semi", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "Fit {AC} into the semicircle {ABC|semiT} equal to the straight line LO, not being greater than the diameter {AB}.",
              visible: LFIT,
              highlighted: ["ACs","AB"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "C",   name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "ACs", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "AC fitted equal to LO", ref: "IV.1" }] },
            { text: "Join {CB|CBs}.",
              visible: LJN,
              highlighted: ["CBs"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "CBs", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "ABC", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] } },
            { text: "Since the angle {ACB|angACB} is an angle in the semicircle {ACB|semiT}, therefore the angle {ACB|angACB} is right.",
              visible: LRT,
              highlighted: ["angACB","semiT"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angACB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "the angle in a semicircle is right", ref: "III.31" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB}.",
              visible: LRT,
              highlighted: ["AB","AC","CB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AB² = AC² + CB²", ref: "I.47" }] },
            { text: "Hence the square on {AB} equals the square on {AC} minus the square on {CB}.",
              visible: LRT,
              highlighted: ["AB","AC","CB"] },
            { text: "But {AC} equals LO. Therefore the square on {AB} equals the square on LO minus the square on {CB}.",
              visible: LRT,
              highlighted: ["AC","AB","CB"] },
            { text: "Therefore if we cut off OR equal to {BC},",
              visible: LRT,
              highlighted: ["CB"] },
            { text: "then the square on {AB} will equal the square on LO minus the square on OR.",
              visible: LRT,
              highlighted: ["AB"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.F." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "Lemma") return "#lemma";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });
</script>
</figure>

Set out the straight lines {AB} and *LO,* and let {AB} be the greater. Describe the semicircle {ABC|semiT} on {AB}. Fit {AC} into the semicircle {ABC|semiT} equal to the straight line *LO,* not being greater than the diameter {AB}. Join {CB}. [!just IV.1]

Since the angle {ACB|angACB} is an angle in the semicircle {ACB|semiT}, therefore the angle {ACB|angACB} is right. [!just III.31]

Therefore the square on {AB} equals the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB}. [!just I.47]

Hence the square on {AB} equals the square on {AC} minus the square on {CB}. But {AC} equals *LO.* Therefore the square on {AB} equals the square on *LO* minus the square on {CB}. Therefore if we cut off *OR* equal to {BC}, then the square on {AB} will equal the square on *LO* minus the square on *OR.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
----
guide:

This proposition shows that the necessary conditions for constructing a solid angle found in @XI.20 (the sum any two angles must be less than the third) and @XI.21 (the sum of the three angles must be less than four right angles) are, in fact, sufficient. It is interesting to see how parts of the construction disappear as the angles {B}, {E}, and {H} grow so that these conditions fail.

This proposition completes the introductory portion of Book XI. Most of the remainder deals with parallelepipedal solids and their properties.

#### About the proof

